    "lawCode": "Amd §1004, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S146\n\nSPONSOR: THOMAS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to including the\npromotion and expansion of cricket in the state of New York to the\njurisdiction of the state athletic commission\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo include cricket in the New York State Athletic Commission (NYSAC).\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n  Section 1 amends section 1004 of the general business law, as added by\nthe  Chapter  32 of the laws of 2016, to modify the purpose of NYSAC and\nadd cricket as a sport that NYSAC maintains jurisdiction over.\n\n  Section 2 establishes the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe game of cricket was first played in England in the 14th century, and\nby the 18th century, cricket became a  national  phenomenon.  The  first\nwritten  rules  of  the game were adopted in 1744. The first report of a\ncricket match in North America was in 1751, when the  New  York  Gazette\nreported  a  match between teams from London and New York City. In 1844,\nthe United States and Canada faced off in a cricket game  that  was  the\nfirst international sporting event in the modem world.\n\nCricket  has  come a long way since the 19th century. Today, billions of\npeople that live across continents and regions ranging from the Caribbe-\nan, to Europe, to Africa and South Asia play cricket. In  North  America\ncricket  is  gaining popularity, thanks to the huge influx of immigrants\nfrom these regions. In 2005,  New  York  City  mayor  Michael  Bloomberg\nannounced  a  plan  to  build a $1.5 million cricket pitch in St. Albans\nPark, Queens.\n\nNew York State takes pride in its diversity, and New York  City  is  the\nworld's most cosmopolitan city -- both of which makes for an ideal place\nfor  a  game such as cricket to thrive. There is a great deal of support\nand enthusiasm for the game amongst New Yorkers who seek to  expand  the\ngame,  which is hand in hand with the internationalism of the state. New\nYork serves as a cultural hub for many regions  of  the  world,  cricket\nwill be yet another addition to that multicultural New York.\n\n \nPURPOSE OF AMENDMENT:\n\nThe  purpose  of  the amendment is to change section 1004 of the general\nbusiness law, the jurisdiction of the commission,  to  include  cricket.\nThe previous version of the bill was amending section 1003 of the gener-\nal business law.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2020:  S.7764-A  (Thomas)-  Referred to Rules Committee/A.9823-A (Rozie)\nPassed the Assembly.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
